      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The incidence of pain associated with the injection of propofol still remains a problem. This study sought to examine the analgesic effects of inhaled nitrous oxide in oxygen on the prevention of propofol injection pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Nitrous oxide in oxygen was compared with a lidocaine (20 mg)-propofol mixture and with propofol alone (control) in a prospective, randomized, observer-blinded study. ASA I and II patients (n = 135) scheduled for elective surgical procedures were studied. A standard propofol injection technique and scoring system to measure the pain on injection was used."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Demographic variables were similar between the study groups. Without analgesia (control) 26 of 45 patients (58%) reported pain on injection compared with 11 of 45 patients (24%) in both the nitrous oxide (95% CI: 14-52%, P = 0.001) and lidocaine groups (95% CI: 14-52%, P = 0.001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The inhalation of a nitrous oxide/oxygen mixture significantly reduces the incidence of pain during propofol injection. This therapeutic stratagem was as effective as a lidocaine-propofol mixture."
        }
      ]},
